var OPHEP_S13_DRAFT = [

{ id:"ophep-retina-1", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A retinal break without detachment can be treated by:", options:["Vitrectomy","Buckle","Argon laser","All of the above"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"A rhegmatogenous detachment begins when a tear or break develops in the retina and vitreous fluid seeps through it into the subretinal space (L20, slide 6). The operations in the list are the ones taught for a detachment that has already happened. Vitrectomy removes the vitreous gel and replaces it with a gas or oil bubble to push the retina back, scleral buckling places a silicone band around the eye to push the eye wall inward, and pneumatic retinopexy injects a gas bubble to seal the tear from the inside, often followed by laser or cryotherapy (L20, slide 14). When the retina is still attached, sealing the break with laser is enough, and argon laser does this by making a scar around the break that holds the retina down (not taken from the course material).\n\nVitrectomy is wrong because it is an operation for an established detachment, in which the gel is removed and a gas or oil bubble pushes the retina back into place (L20, slide 14). Buckle is wrong for the same reason: the silicone band indents the eye wall to help a detached retina reattach (L20, slide 14). All of the above is wrong because two of its three parts are operations for a detached retina, not for a break alone.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Argon laser photocoagulation seals small retinal breaks to prevent detachment. Vitrectomy or scleral buckling is reserved for cases with established retinal detachment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Treat a retinal break that has not yet detached the retina with argon laser, keeping vitrectomy and scleral buckling for established detachment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.900" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-2", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"The fundus picture of central retinal artery occlusion does not include:", options:["Marked narrowing of retinal arterioles","Numerous flame shaped hemorrhages","Opaque white retina","Cherry red spot"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) is drawn in the lecture with visual acuity below 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect, retinal whitening, a cherry-red spot at the macula, arteriolar and venular narrowing, sludging and segmentation of the blood column (cattle-trucking) and a very poor prognosis (L19.2). Haemorrhage is not on that list. Flame-shaped and dot-blot haemorrhages belong to acute branch retinal vein occlusion, and extensive retinal haemorrhages to ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion (L19.2). A blocked vein produces them because venous occlusion causes stagnation, hypoxia and raised extravascular pressure, ending in oedema and haemorrhage (L19.2).\n\nMarked narrowing of retinal arterioles is wrong because arteriolar and venular narrowing is part of the CRAO picture (L19.2). Opaque white retina is wrong because retinal whitening is a CRAO sign (L19.2). Cherry red spot is wrong because a cherry-red spot at the macula is listed among the CRAO signs (L19.2); it shows up because the macula keeps its colour against the whitened retina around it (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) causes sudden vision loss with a pale opaque retina and a cherry red spot, along with narrow arterioles. Flame-shaped hemorrhages are characteristic of venous occlusion, not arterial occlusion.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recall the fundus signs of central retinal artery occlusion and separate them from the flame-shaped haemorrhages of venous occlusion.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.902" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-3", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"Sudden diminution of vision occurs in:", options:["Central retinal vein thrombosis","Central retinal artery occlusion","Massive vitreous hemorrhage","Acute angle closure glaucoma"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) is the picture in which the retinal circulation stops. The lecture gives visual acuity below 6/60 with a marked afferent pupillary defect, retinal whitening and a cherry-red spot at the macula (L19.2); the full fundus picture is set out in ophep-retina-2. The artery is blocked by embolism or by vaso-obliteration, and atherosclerosis is the most common cause of central artery occlusion (L19.2). Sudden profound loss of vision is the presentation of the lecture's CRAO case (L31,32, slides 4 and 9); it is abrupt because the retinal circulation stops at once (not taken from the course material).\n\nCentral retinal vein thrombosis is wrong because a vein occlusion is drawn as stagnation with oedema and haemorrhage, with venous tortuosity and dilatation (L19.2). Only the ischaemic form drops vision below 6/60, while the non-ischaemic form is graded VA > CF (L19.2). Massive vitreous hemorrhage is wrong because vitreous haemorrhage is a complication of proliferative retinopathy, stage 4 of proliferative sickle-cell retinopathy (L19.2) and an indication for vitreoretinal surgery when severe and persistent (L19.1); it is blood in the vitreous, not a block of the retinal artery. Acute angle closure glaucoma is wrong because the attack is painful, with pain graded ++++ and coloured haloes around light (L12), and the printed explanation gives painless loss as the mark of CRAO.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Sudden, painless loss of vision is characteristic of central retinal artery occlusion due to acute interruption of retinal blood flow.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Link sudden loss of vision to central retinal artery occlusion, and separate it from vein occlusion, vitreous haemorrhage and acute angle closure glaucoma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.904" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-4", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"All of the following can be symptoms of a rhegmatogenous retinal detachment EXCEPT:", options:["Photopsia","Floaters","Field defect","Night blindness"],
  answer:3, image:"q-oph-ep-907", imgAlt:"Slide from a lecture, text list",
  explanation:"A rhegmatogenous retinal detachment (RRD) presents with floaters, flashes of light and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision, often sudden and rapid (L20, slide 11). The fundus figure for RRD labels flashes of light, dark floaters and a curtain (L20, slide 12). The slide in the figure lists the same symptoms: acute onset, flashes of light (photopsia), floaters, field defect as a curtain of blackness or peripheral field loss, and failed central vision if the macula is involved.\n\nPhotopsia is wrong because flashes of light are a listed RRD symptom (L20, slides 11 and 12). Floaters is wrong because floaters head the RRD symptom list (L20, slide 11). Field defect is wrong because the curtain in the peripheral vision is a field defect (L20, slide 11). Night blindness is the answer because no RRD slide lists it. It is the complaint of retinitis pigmentosa, a degeneration of the rod photoreceptors that serve night vision (not taken from the course material). The examination lecture shows night blindness only as a bare heading (L1,2, slide 80).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment presents with flashes (photopsia), floaters, and field defects  not night blindness. Night blindness occurs in retinitis pigmentosa.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recall that rhegmatogenous retinal detachment presents with flashes, floaters and a field defect, while night blindness belongs to retinitis pigmentosa.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.906" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-5", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"The retina is composed of:", options:["6 layers","8 layers","9 layers","10 layers"],
  answer:3, image:"q-oph-ep-909", imgAlt:"Labelled schematic, eye in section beside an enlarged cross-section of its wall",
  explanation:"The retina is counted as ten layers, running from the retinal pigment epithelium out to the internal limiting membrane. The layered diagram in the ocular anatomy lecture names them from the vitreal side to the scleral side: internal limiting membrane, nerve fiber layer, ganglion cell layer, inner plexiform layer, inner nuclear layer, outer plexiform layer, outer nuclear layer, external limiting membrane, photoreceptor layer and retinal pigment epithelium (L1,2, slide 31). That is ten names. The same slide also labels the photoreceptor outer segment, Bruch's membrane and the choroid, which are a part of the photoreceptor layer and tissue outside the retina (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe detachment lecture draws the same anatomy: light enters at the nerve fibre layer side and reaches the rods and cones last, so the retina is inverted, and the ganglion, amacrine, bipolar and horizontal cells, the rods and cones, the pigment epithelium and the choroid are all labelled (L20, slide 4). The figure shows a labelled schematic of the retina beside an enlarged cross-section of the wall of the eye, with the same cell types, the choroid and the sclera named. It prints no layer count.\n\n6 layers, 8 layers and 9 layers are wrong because each count is smaller than the ten names on the layered diagram, so each leaves some of them out.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> The retina consists of 10 layers, from the retinal pigment epithelium to the internal limiting membrane. Each layer plays a specific role in visual processing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recall that the retina is built of ten layers, from the retinal pigment epithelium to the internal limiting membrane.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.908" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-6", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 60-year-old patient complains of sudden painless vision loss. He has a history of transient ischemic attacks. Ophthalmoscopy shows a cherry-red spot. What is the diagnosis?", options:["Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)","Central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO)","Retinal detachment","Diabetic retinopathy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A cherry-red spot at the macula is a sign of central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO), drawn in the lecture with visual acuity below 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect, retinal whitening and narrowing of arterioles and venules (L19.2); the full picture is set out in ophep-retina-2. The artery is blocked by embolism, from the heart or the carotid, or by vaso-obliteration, and atherosclerosis is the most common cause of central artery occlusion (L19.2). Fibrinoplatelet emboli may cause amaurosis fugax and occasionally permanent obstruction (L19.2). A history of transient ischemic attacks points to atherosclerotic disease of the carotid or the heart as the likely source of an embolus (not taken from the course material).\n\nCentral retinal vein occlusion is wrong because the venous picture is venous tortuosity and dilatation, retinal haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and disc oedema, with no cherry-red spot (L19.2). Retinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes of light and a curtain in the peripheral vision (L20, slide 11). Diabetic retinopathy is wrong because its signs are microaneurysms, dot and blot haemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton-wool spots and neovascularization (L19.1).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> CRAO presents with sudden painless vision loss and a cherry-red spot on fundoscopy due to retinal ischemia. It is an ophthalmic emergency related to embolic or vascular causes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Diagnose central retinal artery occlusion from sudden painless vision loss and a cherry-red spot in a patient with an embolic source.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.910" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-7", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 20-year-old patient has gradual night vision loss, pale optic disc, and attenuated vessels. Which tests help diagnose?", options:["Electrophysiological tests","OCT","Fundus fluorescein angiography","Visual field test"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The stem is retinitis pigmentosa: night vision lost gradually, a pale disc and attenuated vessels (not taken from the course material). The optic nerve lecture shows a fundus photograph that it does not name, with a waxy pale disc, grossly attenuated vessels and coarse black pigment clumps over the mid-periphery, sparing the posterior pole (L21, slide 16). The electroretinogram (ERG) is the electrophysiological test, and it confirms the diagnosis because it records reduced activity of the photoreceptors, the cells that fail first in this disease (not taken from the course material).\n\nOCT is wrong because it images the retina in cross-section and shows structure, not the electrical response of the photoreceptors (not taken from the course material). Fundus fluorescein angiography is wrong because it shows perfusion and leakage, for example the early blocked and late hyperfluorescent picture of branch vein occlusion or the non-filling of vessels in central artery occlusion (L19.2), and leakage from diabetic maculopathy (L19.1). Visual field test is wrong because perimetry, manual or automated, maps the field the patient already has (L1,2, slide 123) but does not confirm the disease (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Retinitis pigmentosa causes night blindness, attenuated vessels, and pale disc. Electroretinography confirms diagnosis by showing reduced photoreceptor activity.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Confirm retinitis pigmentosa with electrophysiology (electroretinography) and separate it from imaging, angiography and field testing.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.912" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-8", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"Age-related macular degeneration can be monitored at home by:", options:["Amsler grid","Snellen chart","Confrontation test","Ishihara plates"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The Amsler grid tests the central 10 degrees of the visual field (L1,2, slide 123), and it is listed among the tests of macular function (L35,36). Metamorphopsia is one of the listed macular symptoms, and age-related macular degeneration (AMD) affects central vision (L35,36). Drusen alone give near-normal acuity with minimal metamorphopsia (L35,36). The grid is a printed chart that the patient looks at with one eye, so it needs no equipment and no examiner, and lines that turn wavy or go missing show that the disease has moved (not taken from the course material).\n\nSnellen chart is wrong because it is one of the three distance charts shown in the examination lecture, with the E chart and the Landolt C chart (L1,2, slide 113); it measures acuity, not distortion of straight lines. Confrontation test is wrong because it is the bedside field test for a patient with acuity of 1/60 or better (L1,2, slide 123), and the examiner's own field is the reference (L1,2, slide 124). Ishihara plates is wrong because they test colour vision (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> The Amsler grid detects central visual distortion (metamorphopsia) and helps patients with macular degeneration monitor progression at home.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Name the Amsler grid as the home test for central distortion in age-related macular degeneration and separate it from acuity, confrontation and colour tests.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.914" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-9", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 62-year-old diabetic woman with pre-existing proliferative diabetic retinopathy (PDR) is at risk of developing which of the following?", options:["Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Tractional retinal detachment","Exudative retinal detachment","All of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Advanced diabetic retinopathy is the most common cause of tractional retinal detachment, in which scar tissue or membranes pull the retina away from the back of the eye; unlike a rhegmatogenous detachment there is typically no initial tear (L20, slide 7). The lecture figure names proliferative diabetic retinopathy, with fibrovascular membranes or scar tissue exerting a mechanical pulling force (L20, slide 10). Proliferative retinopathy is defined by neovascularization at the disc (NVD) or elsewhere (NVE), and progressive proliferation despite laser, or a detachment involving the macula, are listed indications for vitreoretinal surgery (L19.1). Tractional detachment gives gradual vision loss with distorted or wavy vision (L20, slide 11) and is treated by vitrectomy with peeling of the fibrovascular membranes, alongside control of the diabetic retinopathy (L20, slide 15).\n\nThe three types are set side by side in the lecture (L20, slide 9). Rhegmatogenous: main cause a retinal tear or break, surface corrugated or wrinkled, common risk myopia and ageing. Tractional: main cause scar tissue pulling, surface smooth and taut, common risk diabetes. Exudative: main cause fluid leakage, surface smooth or bullous, common risk inflammation and tumours.\n\nRhegmatogenous is wrong because it needs a tear or break, with ageing, high myopia or trauma as causes (L20, slide 6), and it presents with floaters, flashes and a curtain, often suddenly (L20, slide 11). Exudative is wrong because fluid builds up behind the retina without any tear or traction, from inflammatory disease such as uveitis, tumours such as choroidal melanoma, or severe hypertension (L20, slide 8). All of the above is wrong because the three types have three different causes, and the cause in the stem, proliferative diabetic retinopathy, belongs to the tractional type (L20, slides 7 and 9).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Tractional retinal detachment (TrRD) → Caused by vitreous, epiretinal, or subretinal membrane pulling the neurosensory retina away from the underlying RPE. It is found in conditions such as DR, CRVO, sickle cell disease, ROP, and ocular trauma.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Predict tractional retinal detachment in proliferative diabetic retinopathy and separate the three types of detachment by cause and retinal surface.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.916" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-10", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 65-year-old woman complains of sudden onset of flashes and floaters. Visual field examination revealed peripheral field loss. She was also found to have low IOP in the affected eye. Which of the following is an appropriate line of management for that patient?", options:["Anti-VEGF","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","Intravitreal steroids","Pneumatic retinopexy"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Flashes and floaters of sudden onset with peripheral field loss are the rhegmatogenous detachment picture: floaters, flashes of light and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision, often sudden and rapid (L20, slide 11). Low pressure in the affected eye fits a retinal detachment as well (not taken from the course material). Surgery is almost always required to seal the break (L20, slide 14). The options taught are vitrectomy, in which the gel is replaced by a gas or oil bubble, scleral buckling with a silicone band, and pneumatic retinopexy, in which a gas bubble is injected to seal the tear from the inside, often followed by laser or cryotherapy (L20, slide 14). Of the four options only pneumatic retinopexy is one of them.\n\nAnti-VEGF is wrong because anti-VEGF injections are taught for exudative detachment, to stop leaking from abnormal vessels (L20, slide 16), and for wet macular degeneration (L35,36). Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor is wrong because it is a glaucoma drug that lowers pressure by decreasing aqueous humour production (L11), and the pressure here is already low. Intravitreal steroids is wrong because corticosteroids are the medical therapy for inflammatory causes of exudative detachment, uveitis and VKH syndrome (L20, slide 16).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Treatment of RRD\n> Scleral buckle procedure.\n> Pneumatic retinopexy.\n> Vitrectomy with the injection of gas to tamponade (support) the retina in place or silicone oil in cases of recurrent detachment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Manage a rhegmatogenous retinal detachment surgically, with pneumatic retinopexy among the procedures, rather than with anti-VEGF, steroids or glaucoma drugs.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.918" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-11", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"Which of the following is a well-known cause of tractional retinal detachment?", options:["Malignant hypertension","Posterior uveitis","High myopia","Sickle cell disease"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Sickle cell disease is named in the detachment lecture as a cause of tractional retinal detachment, where scar tissue or membranes pull the retina away from the back of the eye (L20, slide 7). The ocular manifestations lecture gives the route: intravascular sickling leads to thrombosis and arteriolar occlusion, then capillary nonperfusion, followed by neovascularization and retinal detachment (L23). The stages of proliferative sickle-cell retinopathy end with stage 5, fibrovascular proliferation and traction (L19.2). The three types of detachment are compared in ophep-retina-9, where a diabetic patient with proliferative retinopathy is the tractional example.\n\nMalignant hypertension is wrong because a severe rise in blood pressure damages the vessel wall and gives exudates, cotton-wool spots, flame-shaped haemorrhages and even subretinal fluid (L23), the route to exudative detachment (L20, slide 8). Posterior uveitis is wrong because inflammatory disease such as uveitis leaks fluid from the choroid into the subretinal space, which is exudative detachment (L20, slide 8). High myopia is wrong because it is a cause of rhegmatogenous detachment, with ageing and trauma (L20, slide 6).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Tractional retinal detachment (TrRD) → Caused by vitreous, epiretinal, or subretinal membrane pulling the neurosensory retina away from the underlying RPE. It is found in conditions such as DR, CRVO, sickle cell disease, ROP, and ocular trauma.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise sickle cell disease as a cause of tractional retinal detachment and place hypertension, uveitis and myopia in the other two types.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.920" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-12", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 60-year-old hypertensive male patient was found by an ophthalmologist to have a light reflex that occupies most of the width of the vessels (copper-wire arterioles). Fundoscopy revealed retinal hemorrhages and exudates. Which of the following is a potential complication for such a patient?", options:["Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Tractional retinal detachment","Exudative retinal detachment","All of the above"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Arteriolar sclerosis gives an increase in the light reflex of the arteriole, described as copper wire or silver wire, and a severe rise in blood pressure causes exudates, cotton-wool spots, flame-shaped haemorrhages and even subretinal fluid (L23). Fluid from the choroid seeping into the subretinal space, with no tear and no traction, is exudative detachment, and severe hypertension is one of its named causes with inflammatory disease and tumours (L20, slide 8). Treatment is aimed at the cause, hypertension control among them (L20, slide 16). Compare ophep-retina-9, the same four options with a different stem: there a diabetic woman with proliferative diabetic retinopathy is at risk of tractional detachment, while here the hypertensive man is at risk of the exudative type.\n\nRhegmatogenous is wrong because it needs a retinal tear or break, with ageing, high myopia or trauma as the causes (L20, slide 6). Tractional is wrong because it is scar tissue pulling on the retina, most commonly in advanced diabetic retinopathy, and also in sickle cell disease or after trauma (L20, slide 7). All of the above is wrong because the hypertensive picture in the stem leads to one of the three types, not all of them.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Exudative retinal detachment → Caused by damage to the RPE resulting in fluid accumulation in the Subretinal space. Main causes are malignant hypertension, toxaemia of pregnancy, posterior uveitis, and intraocular tumours.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Predict exudative retinal detachment as the complication of severe hypertensive retinopathy and separate it from rhegmatogenous and tractional detachment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.922" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-13", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 65-year-old man presents with sudden painless severe monocular vision loss. Ophthalmoscopic examination revealed cherry red spots together with venous stasis. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", options:["CRAO","CRVO","Retinal detachment","AMD","Retinitis pigmentosa"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A cherry-red spot in a sudden, painless, severe loss of one eye is central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO). The lecture draws CRAO with visual acuity below 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect, retinal whitening, a cherry-red spot at the macula, arteriolar and venular narrowing, and sludging and segmentation of the blood column (cattle-trucking), with a very poor prognosis (L19.2). A stagnant column of blood in the vessels is therefore part of the arterial picture, and it does not turn the diagnosis into a vein occlusion. Compare ophep-retina-2 for the full sign list and ophep-retina-3 for the sudden loss.\n\nCRVO is wrong because the vein occlusion picture is venous tortuosity and dilatation, retinal haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and disc oedema, with no cherry-red spot (L19.2). Retinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes of light and a curtain in the peripheral vision (L20, slide 11). AMD is wrong because age-related macular degeneration affects central vision through drusen and metamorphopsia, and its dry type is a slow progressive atrophy of the RPE and photoreceptors (L35,36). Retinitis pigmentosa is wrong because its complaint is night blindness (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose central retinal artery occlusion from a cherry-red spot with a stagnant blood column and separate it from vein occlusion, detachment, macular degeneration and retinitis pigmentosa.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.924" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-14", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 57-year-old diabetic and hypertensive male patient complained of acute painless loss of vision in his left eye. Ophthalmoscopy revealed (blood and thunder appearance) together with cotton wool spots and diffuse retinal hemorrhages. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", options:["CRAO","CVRO","Retinal detachment","Retinitis pigmentosa"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The stem is a central retinal vein occlusion, with extensive haemorrhages and cotton-wool spots in the eye of a diabetic, hypertensive patient. The lecture draws the ischaemic form with visual acuity below 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect, marked venous tortuosity and engorgement, extensive retinal haemorrhages, variable cotton-wool spots, severe disc oedema, macular ischaemia and a very poor prognosis, with rubeosis irides in 50% (L19.2). The non-ischaemic form is milder, with mild to moderate haemorrhages and disc oedema and VA > CF, and it may subsequently convert to ischaemic (L19.2). The 'blood and thunder' appearance is the name for this picture of widespread haemorrhage along engorged veins (not taken from the course material). Venous occlusion causes stagnation, hypoxia and raised extravascular pressure, ending in oedema and haemorrhage (L19.2). The systemic predisposing factors are increasing age, hypertension, diabetes and abnormalities of coagulation, and the ocular ones raised intraocular pressure and periphlebitis (L19.2), so a diabetic and hypertensive patient of 57 fits.\n\nCentral retinal artery occlusion is wrong because it whitens the retina, with a cherry-red spot and narrowed vessels (L19.2). Retinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes and a curtain in the peripheral vision (L20, slide 11). Retinitis pigmentosa is wrong because its complaint is night blindness (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose central retinal vein occlusion from extensive haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and engorged veins in a patient with diabetes and hypertension.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.926" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-15", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 61-year-old woman complains of seeing straight objects as if they were wavy. History was taken and it showed that the patient was a heavy smoker. Ophthalmoscopy revealed drusen formation. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?", options:["Retinal detachment","CRVO","BRVO","Retinitis pigmentosa","AMD"],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"The stem is age-related macular degeneration (AMD). Seeing straight lines as wavy is metamorphopsia (not taken from the course material), one of the listed macular symptoms (L35,36). Drusen are hyaline nodules, or colloid bodies, deposited in Bruch's membrane, and drusen alone give normal or near-normal acuity with minimal metamorphopsia (L35,36). AMD has a dry type, 90%, a slow progressive atrophy of the RPE and photoreceptors, and a wet type, 10%, with RPE detachment and choroidal neovascularization (L35,36). Smoking heads the list of risk factors that a patient can control (L35,36). Compare ophep-retina-13, which shares four of these five options (CRVO, retinal detachment, retinitis pigmentosa and AMD) but asks about a sudden painless loss with a cherry-red spot, where central retinal artery occlusion is the answer.\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes and a curtain (L20, slide 11). The lecture also lists distorted or wavy vision for tractional detachment, but that is a gradual loss from scar tissue pulling on the retina in conditions such as advanced diabetic retinopathy (L20, slides 7 and 11), and drusen are not part of it. CRVO is wrong because the vein occlusion picture is venous tortuosity and dilatation, haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and disc oedema (L19.2). BRVO is wrong because acute branch vein occlusion shows venous tortuosity, flame-shaped and dot-blot haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and retinal oedema (L19.2). Retinitis pigmentosa is wrong because its complaint is night blindness (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose age-related macular degeneration from metamorphopsia, smoking and drusen, and separate it from detachment, vein occlusion and retinitis pigmentosa.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.928" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-17", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 45-year-old male patient was diagnosed with complete central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO). However, vision was partially intact. Which of the following is the cause for that partial preservation of vision in that patient?", options:["Development of collaterals that bypass the occluded segment","Cilioretinal artery","Lucid interval with terminal loss of vision","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) is drawn with visual acuity below 6/60 (L19.2), so any vision left needs a second blood supply to part of the retina. The cilioretinal artery derives from the posterior ciliary circulation and is present in about 30% of individuals (L19.2). On fluorescein angiography of CRAO the cilioretinal artery fills early while the other vessels do not fill (L19.2). The retina that this artery supplies therefore keeps its circulation when the central artery is blocked (not taken from the course material). The CRAO fundus picture is set out in ophep-retina-2.\n\nDevelopment of collaterals that bypass the occluded segment is wrong because the lecture places collaterals in old branch retinal vein occlusion, with vascular sheathing (L19.2), and not as a route around a blocked central artery. Lucid interval with terminal loss of vision is wrong because it is not a feature of retinal artery occlusion in the lecture (L19.2). None of the above is wrong because the cilioretinal artery is one of the options and the lecture names it.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Explain partial preservation of vision in central retinal artery occlusion by a cilioretinal artery, present in about 30% of individuals.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.932" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-18", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 25-year-old man complains of night blindness and decreased peripheral vision. What is the most likely diagnosis?", options:["CRAO","BRAO","Retinal detachment","Retinitis pigmentosa"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Night blindness with decreasing peripheral vision in a young adult is retinitis pigmentosa (not taken from the course material). The fundus photograph in the optic nerve lecture shows coarse black pigment clumps over the mid-periphery, sparing the posterior pole, with a waxy pale disc and grossly attenuated vessels (L21, slide 16); the pigment in the mid-periphery matches the loss of peripheral before central vision (not taken from the course material). Compare ophep-retina-7, the same disease asked as a test, where electrophysiology is the answer. Compare also ophep-retina-14, which shares three of these four options (central retinal artery occlusion, retinal detachment, retinitis pigmentosa) with a vein occlusion as the fourth.\n\nCRAO is wrong because central artery occlusion is drawn with visual acuity below 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect, retinal whitening and a cherry-red spot (L19.2). BRAO is wrong because a branch artery occlusion gives variable acuity, a mild or absent afferent pupillary defect, retinal whitening and arteriolar narrowing (L19.2). Retinal detachment is wrong because a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision comes with floaters and flashes of light (L20, slide 11), and the stem has neither.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose retinitis pigmentosa from night blindness with decreasing peripheral vision and separate it from artery occlusion and detachment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.934" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-19", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"Which of the following increases the risk of central retinal vein occlusion?", options:["Beta blockers","Steroids","Oral contraceptives","Anemia"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The lecture lists the predisposing factors for retinal vein occlusion: systemic ones are increasing age, hypertension, diabetes and abnormalities of coagulation, and ocular ones are raised intraocular pressure and periphlebitis (L19.2). Oral contraceptives raise the tendency of the blood to clot (not taken from the course material), which brings them under the abnormalities of coagulation on that list. The picture and pathophysiology of the occlusion are set out in ophep-retina-14.\n\nBeta blockers is wrong because they are not on the list, and in the glaucoma lecture they lower pressure by decreasing aqueous humour production (L11), the opposite of the raised pressure that is on the list. Steroids is wrong because in the retinal lectures corticosteroids are a treatment, used for inflammatory causes of exudative detachment (L20, slide 16), and they are not a listed predisposing factor. Anemia is wrong because anaemia produces its own retinopathy of haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and venous tortuosity (L19.2), which is not a predisposing factor for vein occlusion.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recall the risk factors for central retinal vein occlusion and place oral contraceptives among the abnormalities of coagulation.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.936" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-20", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 52 year old man presents with sudden complete loss of vision from the right eye. He also had right-sided headaches, worse on chewing. On fundoscopy, the retina was pale with a cherry red spot in the macular region. What is the SINGLE most likely cause of vision loss?", options:["Central retinal artery occlusion","Central retinal vein occlusion","Branch retinal artery occlusion","Branch retinal vein occlusion","Open angle glaucoma"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the macula and complete loss of vision is central retinal artery occlusion, drawn with visual acuity below 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect and retinal whitening (L19.2); the sign list is in ophep-retina-2. Headache worse on chewing is jaw claudication and points to giant cell arteritis (not taken from the course material). The lecture links giant cell arteritis to arterial occlusion in the eye: a cilioretinal artery occlusion combined with anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy occurs in elderly patients with giant cell arteritis, with a very poor prognosis (L19.2). Vaso-obliteration is one of the two causes of retinal artery occlusion, with embolism (L19.2).\n\nCentral retinal vein occlusion is wrong because the venous picture is tortuous, engorged veins with extensive haemorrhage and disc oedema (L19.2), not a pale retina. Branch retinal artery occlusion is wrong because it gives variable acuity and a mild or absent afferent pupillary defect (L19.2), not the complete loss in the stem. Branch retinal vein occlusion is wrong because it shows venous tortuosity and dilatation, flame-shaped and dot-blot haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and retinal oedema (L19.2). Open angle glaucoma is wrong because it is most commonly asymptomatic, and peripheral field loss and diminution of vision come only in advanced disease (L11).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose central retinal artery occlusion from complete visual loss with a pale retina and cherry-red spot, and connect jaw-claudication headache to giant cell arteritis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.938" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-21", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 48 year old man attends clinic for a routine eye check-up. He has a history of type 1 diabetes. Fundoscopy shows neovascularization at the retina. What is the SINGLE most appropriate management?",
  options:["Strict blood glucose control","Review in 12 months","Non urgent referral to specialist","Insulin","Laser photocoagulation"],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"Neovascularization at the retina in a diabetic eye is proliferative diabetic retinopathy, PDR. The new vessels are flat or elevated, and are called NVD when they sit on the disc and NVE when they sit elsewhere (L19.1). PDR affects 5-10% of diabetics (L19.1). The treatment is laser panretinal photocoagulation, indicated for NVD more than 1/3 disc in area, for less extensive NVD with haemorrhage, and for NVE more than 1/2 disc in area with haemorrhage (L19.1). The initial treatment is 2000-3000 burns, with follow-up at 4 to 8 weeks (L19.1). Laser photocoagulation is therefore the answer.\n\nStrict blood glucose control is wrong because tighter control does not remove new vessels that are already present (not taken from the course material). Insulin is wrong for the same reason: it treats the diabetes, not the retinal new vessels (not taken from the course material).\n\nReview in 12 months is wrong because watching without treating is the plan given for severe non-proliferative disease, which has not yet grown new vessels (L19.1, severe NPDR: treatment not required but watch for proliferative disease). Non urgent referral to specialist is wrong because new vessels call for treatment, not a routine appointment (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise retinal neovascularization in a diabetic patient as proliferative diabetic retinopathy and treat it with laser panretinal photocoagulation.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.940" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-22", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 44 year old hypertensive male loses vision in his left eye overnight. There is no pain or redness. On fundoscopy, venous dilation, tortuosity, and retinal haemorrhages are observed. What is the SINGLE most likely cause of his unilateral visual loss?",
  options:["Hypertension retinopathy","Central Retinal Artery Occlusion","Central Retinal Vein Occlusion","Background retinopathy","Retinal detachment"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Central retinal vein occlusion gives venous dilation and tortuosity with retinal haemorrhages. The ischaemic form shows marked venous tortuosity and engorgement, extensive retinal haemorrhages, severe disc oedema and visual acuity below 6/60 (L19.2). The non-ischaemic form shows mild venous tortuosity and dilatation with mild to moderate retinal haemorrhages (L19.2). Hypertension is one of the systemic predisposing factors, together with increasing age, diabetes and abnormalities of coagulation (L19.2). The man here has hypertension and a venous picture in one eye.\n\nHypertension retinopathy is wrong because its lead sign is arteriolar constriction, focal or generalized, with A-V changes (L19.2). Its extravascular signs are flame-shaped haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, a macular star and disc oedema (L19.2). The stem describes veins, not narrowed arterioles.\n\nCentral Retinal Artery Occlusion is wrong because its signs are retinal whitening, a cherry-red spot at the macula and arteriolar and venular narrowing (L19.2), not dilated veins.\n\nBackground retinopathy is wrong because the microaneurysms, dot and blot haemorrhages, hard exudates and retinal oedema of mild non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy (L19.1) do not describe a sudden overnight loss; background retinopathy is the older name for non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes of light and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision (L20, slide 11).\n\nThe vein occlusion questions teach one condition from several angles; ophep-retina-14 is the lowest-numbered of them, and ophep-retina-41 asks the central type again in an 80-year-old.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise central retinal vein occlusion from venous dilation, tortuosity and retinal haemorrhages in one eye, and separate it from artery occlusion, hypertensive and diabetic retinopathy, and retinal detachment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.942" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-23", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 70 year old man with diabetes and hypertension experiences acute painless monocular blindness that resolved after 30 minutes. He describes it as a curtain coming down vertically into one eye. What is the SINGLE most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Giant cell arteritis","Optic neuritis","Lacunar infarct","Pontine haemorrhage","Amaurosis fugax"],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"Amaurosis fugax is a transient, painless loss of vision in one eye, often described as a curtain coming down, and this man’s attack cleared within 30 minutes (not taken from the course material). It is listed among the sudden causes of loss of vision (L1,2, slide 133). Fibrinoplatelet emboli are multiple dull grey particles that occasionally fill the entire lumen and may cause amaurosis fugax and occasionally permanent obstruction (L19.2). Emboli come from the heart or the carotid artery (L19.2). Atherosclerosis is the most common cause of central artery occlusion (L19.2), and diabetes and hypertension are its risk factors (not taken from the course material).\n\nGiant cell arteritis is wrong because it appears in the lecture as the cause when a cilioretinal artery occlusion is combined with anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy in elderly patients, with a very poor prognosis (L19.2). That is vision that stays lost, not vision that returns in 30 minutes.\n\nOptic neuritis is wrong because its features are reduced visual acuity, reduced colour vision and reduced contrast sensitivity, with a field defect and a relative afferent pupillary defect (L21, slide 3), but the loss in optic neuritis lasts days to weeks, not the 30 minutes here (not taken from the course material).\n\nLacunar infarct is wrong because it is a small infarct deep in the brain (not taken from the course material). Pontine haemorrhage is wrong because it is a bleed in the brainstem (not taken from the course material). Neither blinds one eye alone.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise amaurosis fugax as transient painless monocular visual loss from emboli, and separate it from giant cell arteritis, optic neuritis and brain lesions.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.944" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-24", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"60-year-old man notes sudden profound loss of vision in his right eye, with onset one hour ago. The patient has a past medical history of hypertension. On examination, the visual acuity is OD hand motion and OS 6/6. The pupil of the right eye shows RAPD. The direct ophthalmoscope shows that the red reflex is clear in both eyes. Fundus examination in the right eye reveals a white opacified retina with cherry red spot in the macular area. The left eye is normal. No other clinical manifestation. What is your diagnosis?",
  options:["AION","CRAO","Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"These are the findings listed for central retinal artery occlusion: visual acuity below 6/60, a marked relative afferent pupillary defect, retinal whitening, a cherry-red spot at the macula, and arteriolar and venular narrowing (L19.2). A case of almost identical wording, a man with sudden profound loss of vision in one eye, hand motion vision, a right relative afferent pupillary defect, clear red reflexes and a white opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot, is worked to the diagnosis of Central Retinal Artery Occlusion (L31,32, slides 4 to 9). It is listed among the sudden causes of loss of vision (L1,2, slide 133) and among the vision-threatening ocular emergencies (L1,2, slide 135). Atherosclerosis is the most common cause of central artery occlusion (L19.2), and the man has hypertension. Central retinal artery occlusion is taught from other angles in ophep-retina-3.\n\nAION is wrong because its picture is a swollen disc: pale and swollen, with an altitudinal field defect (L21, slides 4 and 9), and a swollen disc with flame-shaped haemorrhages around it (L31,32, slide 22). Here the disc is not described as swollen and the retina is white with a cherry-red macula.\n\nOptic neuritis is wrong because the optic nerve signs listed are reduced acuity, reduced colour vision, reduced contrast sensitivity, a field defect and a relative afferent pupillary defect (L21, slide 3); retinal whitening with a cherry-red spot belongs to artery occlusion (L19.2).\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes of light and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision (L20, slide 11), and a detached retina is corrugated, taut or bullous (L20, slide 9), not white around a cherry-red spot.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Central retinal artery occlusion causes sudden, painless vision loss with a pale retina and cherry-red macula. It’s an ophthalmic emergency.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Diagnose central retinal artery occlusion from sudden profound monocular loss, a marked relative afferent pupillary defect and a white retina with a cherry-red macula.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.946" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-25", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"60-year-old man notes sudden profound loss of vision in his right eye, with onset one hour ago. The patient has a past medical history of hypertension. On examination, the visual acuity is OD hand motion and OS 6/6. The pupil of the right eye shows RAPD. The direct ophthalmoscope shows that the red reflex is clear in both eyes. Fundus examination in the right eye reveals a white opacified retina with cherry red spot in the macular area. The left eye is normal. No other clinical manifestation. Regarding your diagnosis, which of the following would be the most appropriate way of management?",
  options:["Systemic steroid","AC paracentesis","Carotid doppler U/S","Anti-VEGF"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"This is the vignette of ophep-retina-24 asked a second time: the diagnosis there is central retinal artery occlusion, and here the question is management. The immediate aim is to dislodge the embolus and restore retinal perfusion, and anterior chamber paracentesis is one of the measures used for that, with ocular massage (not taken from the course material). AC paracentesis is therefore the answer.\n\nCarotid Doppler U/S is wrong as the first measure because it looks for the source, not for a way to reopen the artery. Emboli come from the heart or the carotid artery (L19.2), and carotid imaging by duplex scanning is high resolution real-time B-scan ultrasonography with Doppler flow analysis (L19.2). It follows the immediate measures, as the box says.\n\nSystemic steroid is wrong because the lecture gives corticosteroids for inflammatory disease such as uveitis or VKH syndrome under exudative detachment (L20, slide 16), not for an embolic artery occlusion.\n\nAnti-VEGF is wrong because anti-VEGF drugs are given to stop leaking from abnormal blood vessels (L20, slide 16), by intravitreal injection in diffuse diabetic maculopathy (L19.1) and for wet age-related macular degeneration (L35,36). They do not reopen an occluded artery (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Immediate measures like ocular massage and anterior chamber paracentesis aim to dislodge the embolus and restore retinal perfusion. Carotid Doppler follows for cause assessment\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Give anterior chamber paracentesis as an immediate measure in central retinal artery occlusion, with carotid Doppler to follow for the source of the embolus.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.948" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-26", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A healthy 48-year-old man complains of seeing \"floating black dots\" in the field of vision of his right eye for 2 days, associated with sensation of flashing light in the periphery of the visual field. Later on, he noticed a disturbance in the temporal field of vision of his right eye like \"curtain coming down\". His visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes. Anterior segment examination is normal. The red reflex of the left eye is normal but in the right eye reveals area of grayish reflex. What is your diagnosis?",
  options:["Vitreous hemorrhage","BRAO","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Tractional retinal detachment"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"A healthy 48-year-old man with floating black dots for 2 days, flashing lights in the periphery and a temporal field disturbance like a curtain coming down is worked to the diagnosis RRD (L31,32, slides 26 to 29). Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment is the most common type. It occurs when a physical tear or break develops in the retina, and vitreous fluid seeps through the tear into the subretinal space, pushing the retina away from its blood supply (L20, slide 6). The key symptoms are floaters, flashes of light and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision, often sudden and rapid (L20, slide 11). In the fundus figure the detached quadrant is drawn pale grey against the orange attached retina (L20, slide 12). Common causes are aging (vitreous shrinkage), high myopia and eye trauma (L20, slide 6).\n\nVitreous hemorrhage is wrong because blood in the vitreous dims the red reflex and hides fundus detail (not taken from the course material); this man keeps 6/6 vision and has a curtain-like temporal field loss.\n\nBRAO is wrong because its signs are variable visual acuity, retinal whitening and arteriolar narrowing (L19.2), not floaters and flashes.\n\nTractional retinal detachment is wrong because the retina is pulled off by scar tissue or membranes, most commonly in advanced diabetic retinopathy, with typically no initial tear (L20, slide 7). Its symptoms are gradual vision loss and distorted or wavy vision (L20, slide 11), and this man is healthy.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Retinal tear allows vitreous fluid to accumulate under the retina, causing flashes and field loss described as a curtain. Urgent surgical repair is needed.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Diagnose rhegmatogenous retinal detachment from floaters, flashes and a curtain over the field in a healthy adult, and separate it from vitreous haemorrhage, branch artery occlusion and tractional detachment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.950" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-27", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"34-year-old woman notes acute vision loss in her left eye. She is in otherwise good health. On examination, her visual acuity is OD 6/6, OS 6/60. Defective color vision is noticed in her left eye. Examination of the pupillary light reflex reveals RAPD in the left eye. The anterior segment examination is normal. The red reflex is clear and fundus examination is normal in both eyes. What is your diagnosis?",
  options:["AION","Optic atrophy","Retrobulbar optic neuritis","Papillitis"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Reduced visual acuity, reduced colour vision, reduced contrast sensitivity, a field defect and a relative afferent pupillary defect are the manifestations of optic nerve dysfunction, with the field defect and RAPD starred as the two signs that localise the lesion to the optic nerve (L21, slide 3). This woman has acuity 6/60, defective colour vision and RAPD in one eye, so the optic nerve is involved. What separates retrobulbar neuritis from papillitis is the disc. Papillitis shows a hyperaemic disc with blurred margins (L21, slide 7) and disc elevation (L21, slide 8). Retrobulbar neuritis is inflammation of the nerve behind the disc, so the fundus looks normal (not taken from the course material), which is what the stem reports in both eyes. The optic nerve is also the site of the central scotoma of acute optic neuritis (L21, slide 4).\n\nAION is wrong because its disc is swollen and pale with an altitudinal field defect (L21, slides 4, 5 and 9), and it appears in the lecture in a 90-year-old with headache, fatigue and weight loss, with a swollen disc and flame-shaped haemorrhages around it (L31,32, slides 20 to 23). This woman is 34 and her disc is normal.\n\nOptic atrophy is wrong because it is the pale-disc end state, with sharp margins and normal vessels in the primary pattern (L21, slide 15); the fundus here is normal.\n\nPapillitis is wrong because the disc is swollen. The lecture case of a 24-year-old woman with sudden loss of vision, a relative afferent pupillary defect and a swollen optic disc is diagnosed as Papillitis (L31,32, slides 12 to 16).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> Optic neuritis causes visual loss and color desaturation with a normal-appearing fundus initially (“the patient sees nothing, the doctor sees nothing”). It’s often associated with multiple sclerosis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Diagnose retrobulbar optic neuritis from reduced acuity, defective colour vision and a relative afferent pupillary defect with a normal fundus, and separate it from papillitis, AION and optic atrophy.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.952" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-28", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"Retinitis Pigmentosa can be:",
  options:["An inherited condition with a “bone spicule” fundus appearance causing loss of central vision","A sporadic condition with a “salt and pepper” fundus appearance causing loss of peripheral vision","An inherited condition with a “bone spicule” fundus appearance causing loss of peripheral vision","A sporadic condition with a \"bone spicule\" fundus\" appearance causing loss of peripheral vision","An inherited condition with a “salt and pepper” fundus appearance causing loss of central vision"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The fundus of retinitis pigmentosa (the slide shows this picture without naming the disease; naming it is not taken from the course material) carries coarse black pigment clumps in a branching, spiky pattern over the mid-periphery of the retina, sparing the posterior pole, with a waxy pale disc and grossly attenuated vessels (L21, slide 16). Those clumps are the bone spicules (not taken from the course material). Because the pigment lies in the periphery, the field lost is the peripheral one and central vision is kept until late (not taken from the course material). Retinitis pigmentosa is among the seven conditions asked about in the history of relatives (L1,2, slide 96), and it is an inherited disease (not taken from the course material). The answer therefore joins inherited, bone spicule and peripheral. Retinitis pigmentosa is also taught in ophep-retina-7.\n\nThe first option is wrong because it puts the loss in central vision; the pigment sits in the periphery and spares the posterior pole (L21, slide 16).\n\nThe second option is wrong twice: retinitis pigmentosa is inherited, not sporadic (not taken from the course material), and its appearance is bone spicule pigment, not a salt and pepper fundus (L21, slide 16).\n\nThe fourth option is wrong because it calls the disease sporadic, whereas it is inherited (not taken from the course material), even though it names the right appearance and the right field loss.\n\nThe fifth option is wrong on two counts: a salt and pepper fundus is not the bone spicule pigment of this disease (L21, slide 16), and the loss is peripheral, not central.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"State that retinitis pigmentosa is an inherited condition with bone spicule pigmentation of the peripheral retina that causes loss of peripheral vision.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.954" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-29", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 78 year old patient presents to A & E complaining of sudden loss of the superior visual field in one eye. On ophthalmoscopy, a yellow plaque is noted in the arterioles of the inferior temporal arcade and the inferior retina appears white and swollen. The investigations most likely to identify the source of this problem are:",
  options:["Carotid doppler ultrasound and echocardiography","CRP and ESR","Ocular coherence tomography (OCT)","MRI brain and orbits","Thyroid function testing"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A plaque in a retinal arteriole with the retina beyond it white and swollen is an embolus blocking a branch artery. Cholesterol emboli, the Hollenhorst plaques, are multiple, bright, refractile crystals, often located at arteriolar bifurcations and frequently asymptomatic (L19.2). Branch retinal artery occlusion shows retina whitening and arteriolar narrowing, with variable visual acuity (L19.2). Emboli come from two places, the heart and the carotid artery (L19.2), so the investigations that find the source look at both. Carotid imaging includes duplex scanning, which is high resolution real-time B-scan ultrasonography with Doppler flow analysis (L19.2). Echocardiography looks at the heart for the cardiac source (not taken from the course material). The white swollen inferior retina explains the loss of the superior field, because the image is inverted on the retina (not taken from the course material).\n\nCRP and ESR are the blood tests for giant cell arteritis (not taken from the course material), and they are wrong because that disease appears in the lecture as the cause of a cilioretinal artery occlusion combined with anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy in elderly patients (L19.2), and here a plaque is visible in an arteriole and the source of the plaque is the question.\n\nOcular coherence tomography (OCT) is wrong because it images the retina and does not search the heart or carotid arteries for the source (not taken from the course material).\n\nMRI brain and orbits is wrong because the carotid imaging technique the lecture lists is magnetic resonance angiography, which also images ischaemic cerebral lesions (L19.2); MRI of the brain and orbits does not look at the heart or the carotid arteries (not taken from the course material).\n\nThyroid function testing is wrong because no thyroid disease is named as a source of retinal emboli in the lecture, whose two sources are cardiac and carotid (L19.2).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Investigate the carotid arteries by Doppler ultrasound and the heart by echocardiography when a retinal artery embolus with a branch artery occlusion is seen.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.956" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-30", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 54 year old woman has had type 1 diabetes for 40 years and presents to the ophthalmology clinic for retinopathy screening by the resident. On dilated fundus examination the resident notes the patient has scattered microaneurysms, several dot hemorrhages and a small area of new blood vessels at an A-V crossing. The resident is likely to:",
  options:["Diagnose no retinopathy and follow the patient up in a year","Diagnose background diabetic retinopathy and follow the patient up in a year","Diagnose clinically significant macula oedema","Diagnose proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Diagnose vitreous hemorrhage"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The findings fall into two groups. Scattered microaneurysms and dot haemorrhages are signs of mild NPDR: microaneurysms usually temporal to the fovea, intraretinal dot and blot haemorrhages, hard exudates and retinal oedema (L19.1). New blood vessels are neovascularization, the defining sign of proliferative diabetic retinopathy: flat or elevated, on the disc as NVD or elsewhere as NVE (L19.1). Once new vessels are present the diagnosis is PDR, whatever else is seen. PDR affects 5-10% of diabetics (L19.1), and this woman has had diabetes for 40 years, since long duration of diabetes is the first adverse risk factor listed (L19.1). The same proliferative picture, treated with laser, is asked in ophep-retina-21.\n\nDiagnose no retinopathy is wrong because microaneurysms, dot haemorrhages and new vessels are all present.\n\nDiagnose background diabetic retinopathy and follow up in a year is wrong because the new vessels put her beyond the non-proliferative stage (L19.1); background retinopathy is the older name for non-proliferative disease (not taken from the course material).\n\nDiagnose clinically significant macula oedema is wrong because it is defined by retinal oedema or hard exudates near the centre of the fovea, or retinal oedema one disc area or larger with any part within one disc diameter of the fovea (L19.1), and the stem records no macular finding.\n\nDiagnose vitreous hemorrhage is wrong because the stem records no bleeding, only new vessels that could bleed later (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose proliferative diabetic retinopathy from new blood vessels in a long-standing diabetic eye, separating it from mild non-proliferative disease, macular oedema and vitreous haemorrhage.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.958" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-31", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"Dry (atrophic) age related macular degeneration:",
  options:["Is characterized by new vessel formation at the macula","Is treatable with intravitreal injections","Is rapidly progressive, leading to loss of central vision","Is rapidly progressive, leading to loss of central and peripheral vision","Is characterized by hard drusen at the early stages"],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"Age-related macular degeneration has two types. The dry type, about 90%, is a slow progressive atrophy of the RPE and photoreceptors, and the wet type, 10%, has RPE detachment and choroidal neovascularization (L35,36). Drusen are hyaline nodules deposited in Bruch’s membrane, small and discrete or larger with irregular shapes and indistinct edges, and patients with drusen alone tend to have normal or near normal visual acuity (L35,36). Drusen are the earliest visible change of the dry type, and hard drusen are the small, discrete ones (not taken from the course material). Hard drusen at the early stages is therefore the answer.\n\nNew vessel formation at the macula is wrong because it is the choroidal neovascularization of the wet type (L35,36).\n\nTreatable with intravitreal injections is wrong because injections of anti VEGF are the treatment of the wet type; for dry AMD the lecture gives dietary supplements of minerals and vitamins, especially vitamin E (L35,36).\n\nRapidly progressive, leading to loss of central vision is wrong because the dry type is a slow progressive atrophy (L35,36).\n\nRapidly progressive, leading to loss of central and peripheral vision is wrong for the same reason, and because macular degeneration affects central vision, making it difficult to read or drive (L35,36).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Describe dry age-related macular degeneration as a slowly progressive atrophy of the RPE and photoreceptors that begins with drusen, and separate it from the wet type.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.960" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-32", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"Wet (exudative) age related macular degeneration:",
  options:["May be treatable with intravitreal injections","Is slowly progressive, leading to loss of central vision over many years","Is a type of ocular diabetic complication","Commonly follows toxoplasmosis infection","Is characterized by flame shaped hemorrhages"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Compare ophep-retina-31, the dry type of the same disease: there the answer is drusen at the early stages, and here the answer is the treatment that belongs to the wet type. The wet type is the 10% form, with RPE detachment and choroidal neovascularization, and its treatment is injections of anti VEGF (L35,36). Anti-VEGF drugs stop leaking from abnormal blood vessels (L20, slide 16), and they are given as an intravitreal injection in diffuse diabetic maculopathy (L19.1). May be treatable with intravitreal injections is the answer.\n\nIs slowly progressive, leading to loss of central vision over many years is wrong because a slow progressive atrophy is the description of the dry type (L35,36).\n\nIs a type of ocular diabetic complication is wrong because diabetic retinopathy is a separate disease with its own pathogenesis of microvascular occlusion and microvascular leakage (L35,36), and macular degeneration is age related.\n\nCommonly follows toxoplasmosis infection is wrong because the risk factors listed are age, race, gender, genetics, smoking, high blood pressure, high cholesterol, poor nutrition, unprotected exposure to sunlight, excessive sugar intake, obesity and a sedentary lifestyle, and toxoplasmosis is not among them (L35,36).\n\nIs characterized by flame shaped hemorrhages is wrong because flame-shaped haemorrhages are a sign of acute branch retinal vein occlusion (L19.2) and of hypertensive retinopathy (L19.2; L23), not of macular degeneration.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Link wet age-related macular degeneration with choroidal neovascularization treated by intravitreal anti-VEGF injections, and separate it from the dry type.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.962" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-33", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 45 year old man who has worn thick glasses since childhood presents to his GP complaining of flashing lights in his right eye and no history of trauma. The most likely serious cause of these symptoms that must be considered for this patient is:",
  options:["Retinal detachment","Temporal arteritis","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Uveitis","Blow out fracture of the orbit"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Retinal detachment is the serious cause to exclude. Flashes of light are one of the key symptoms of rhegmatogenous detachment, together with floaters and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision, often sudden and rapid (L20, slide 11). High myopia is a common cause of it, with aging and eye trauma (L20, slide 6), and looking at the patient’s glasses is a clue to high myopia or high hypermetropia (L1,2, slide 98), so thick glasses since childhood in a man with flashes suggest the myopic cause. Retinal detachment is among the vision-threatening ocular emergencies (L1,2, slide 135).\n\nTemporal arteritis is wrong because giant cell arteritis, another name for it (L1,2, slide 135), appears in the lecture with the artery occlusion and anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy of elderly patients (L19.2), not with flashing lights in a man who has been short-sighted since childhood.\n\nSubarachnoid hemorrhage is wrong because it is a bleed in the space around the brain, not a cause of flashing lights in one eye (not taken from the course material).\n\nUveitis is wrong because in the lecture it is an inflammatory cause of exudative detachment (L20, slide 8), which happens without tears or traction, whereas the myopia here points to the rhegmatogenous type (L20, slides 6 and 9).\n\nBlow out fracture of the orbit is wrong because it follows blunt injury to the orbit (not taken from the course material), and the stem gives no history of trauma.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Consider retinal detachment first when a highly myopic patient reports flashing lights without trauma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.964" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-34", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"Presentation of a central retinal vein occlusion is characterized by:",
  options:["Sudden onset loss of vision with masses of dot hemorrhages in the fundus","Gradual loss of vision with masses of dot hemorrhages in the fundus","Sudden onset loss of vision with masses of flame hemorrhages in the fundus","Gradual loss of vision with masses of flame hemorrhages in the fundus"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture grades central retinal vein occlusion in two forms. The non-ischaemic form shows VA > CF, a mild afferent pupillary defect, mild venous tortuosity and dilatation, mild to moderate retinal haemorrhages, variable cotton-wool spots, mild to moderate disc oedema, chronic macular oedema and a guarded prognosis, and it may subsequently convert to ischaemic (L19.2). The ischaemic form shows VA < 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect, marked venous tortuosity and engorgement, extensive retinal haemorrhages, severe disc oedema and macular ischaemia, with a very poor prognosis and rubeosis irides in 50% (L19.2). Acute branch retinal vein occlusion is listed with flame-shaped and ‘dot-blot’ haemorrhages (L19.2).\n\nThe printed key is the second option, gradual loss of vision with masses of dot hemorrhages in the fundus. The first option pairs sudden onset with dot haemorrhages, the third pairs sudden onset with flame haemorrhages, and the fourth pairs gradual loss with flame haemorrhages, so each differs from the key in the speed of onset, in the type of haemorrhage, or in both.\n\nNote on the printed key: the lecture gives no speed of onset for central retinal vein occlusion, and it describes the haemorrhages of the central occlusion by their amount, mild to moderate or extensive, without naming their shape (L19.2). The answer stays as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recall the lecture’s findings for non-ischaemic and ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion and the printed presentation of the condition.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.966" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-35", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 30-year-old patient with renal failure presents for routine fundus check. Examination reveals a raised optic nerve head with retinal hemorrhages in both eyes. The first line of management is:",
  options:["Ultrasonography","Measurement of the blood pressure","CT scan","Fluorescein angiography"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"A raised optic nerve head with retinal haemorrhages in both eyes in a patient with renal failure is the picture of severe hypertensive retinopathy, so the first step is to measure the blood pressure. A severe acute rise in blood pressure causes fibrinoid necrosis of the vessel wall, resulting in exudates, cotton-wool spots, flame-shaped haemorrhages and even subretinal fluid, and in malignant hypertension, the most severe form of hypertensive retinopathy, the optic disc swelling resembles the swelling seen in papilloedema (L23). In the grading, grade 3 is arteriolar narrowing plus retinal haemorrhages and/or exudates, and grade 4 is grade 3 plus disc swelling (L23). Disc oedema and flame-shaped retinal haemorrhages are listed among the extravascular signs of hypertensive retinopathy (L19.2). Renal failure is a common cause of raised blood pressure (not taken from the course material). The blood pressure is then lowered in a controlled fashion, because a sudden drop in tissue perfusion could result in optic nerve infarction and permanent loss of vision (L23).\n\nUltrasonography is wrong because it images the structures of the eye and does not identify the cause of these signs (not taken from the course material).\n\nCT scan is wrong because it images the head and orbit and does not identify the cause of these signs (not taken from the course material).\n\nFluorescein angiography is wrong as the first step because it shows leakage and non-perfusion in maculopathy and vein occlusion (L19.1; L19.2), and the cause here is one measurement away (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Measure the blood pressure first when bilateral disc swelling with retinal haemorrhages appears in a patient with renal failure, since hypertensive retinopathy is the cause.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.968" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-36", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"67-year-old experienced sudden loss of vision in the left eye three hours ago. His best-corrected visual acuity is 6/6 in the right eye and there is no perception of light in the left eye. The right pupil responds to light directly but not consensually. The left pupil responds to light consensually but not directly. What is your most probable diagnosis?",
  options:["Central retinal vein occlusion","Central retinal artery occlusion","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Diabetic maculopathy"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Sudden loss of vision with a defect of the afferent pupillary pathway on one side points to central retinal artery occlusion. Its findings are visual acuity below 6/60 and a marked afferent pupillary defect, with retinal whitening, a cherry-red spot at the macula and arteriolar and venular narrowing (L19.2). It is listed among the sudden causes of loss of vision (L1,2, slide 133). The pupil pattern here, mirrored to the left eye, is the one written for the artery occlusion case in the lecture: the affected pupil does not respond to light directly but reacts consensually, and the fellow pupil reacts directly but not consensually (L31,32, slides 5 and 6). The same condition is worked through in ophep-retina-24, with its fundus signs.\n\nCentral retinal vein occlusion is wrong because its signs are venous. The ischaemic form has marked venous tortuosity and engorgement and extensive retinal haemorrhages (L19.2). It also carries visual acuity below 6/60 and a marked afferent pupillary defect (L19.2), so acuity and pupil alone do not separate the two, but the stem gives none of the venous signs.\n\nRhegmatogenous retinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes of light and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision (L20, slide 11), and the stem records none of them.\n\nDiabetic maculopathy is wrong because it is leakage, focal, diffuse or cystoid, or an ischaemic maculopathy of a diabetic eye (L19.1), and the stem gives no diabetes and no macular finding.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose central retinal artery occlusion from sudden monocular loss to no perception of light with an afferent pupillary defect of the affected eye.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.970" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-37", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A patient of long-standing diabetes mellitus noticed sudden muscle volatiles. On examination, the red reflex was dim, and no details of fundus could be seen. He might have :",
  options:["Non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Cystoid macular edema","Exudative maculopathy","Vitreous hemorrhage","Central retinal vein occlusion"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"A long-standing diabetic with sudden visual symptoms, a dim red reflex and no view of the fundus has a vitreous haemorrhage, because blood in the vitreous dims the red reflex and hides the fundus (not taken from the course material). Vitreous haemorrhage is listed among the causes of acute loss of vision (L1,2, slide 133). In the diabetic eye it comes from proliferative disease, whose new vessels, NVD and NVE, are flat or elevated (L19.1). Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage is an indication for vitreoretinal surgery, and persistent neovascularization with haemorrhage after laser is poor involution (L19.1).\n\nNon-proliferative diabetic retinopathy is wrong because its signs are microaneurysms, dot and blot haemorrhages, hard exudates and retinal oedema seen on the fundus (L19.1), and here the fundus cannot be seen.\n\nCystoid macular edema is wrong because it is a feature of diffuse diabetic maculopathy, with retinal thickening and variable impairment of visual acuity (L19.1); it is seen on the fundus and does not hide it.\n\nExudative maculopathy is wrong because hard exudates are frequently arranged in clumps or rings (L19.1) that are seen on the fundus, not a screen that hides it.\n\nCentral retinal vein occlusion is wrong because it shows extensive retinal haemorrhages, engorged veins and disc oedema on the fundus (L19.2), and here no fundus detail can be seen.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise vitreous haemorrhage in a long-standing diabetic from sudden symptoms with a dim red reflex and no fundus view.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.972" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-38", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 72-year-old woman complains of altitudinal visual field loss in the left eye over the past few weeks. She denies any eye pain, headache, fatigue, scalp tenderness, and jaw claudication. She has a history of hypertension. Examination shows visual acuity of 1/60, normal anterior segment, retinal hemorrhages, and cotton-wool spots in the superior temporal retina with macular edema. Diagnosis was approved as a case of:",
  options:["Central retinal vein occlusion","Branch retinal artery occlusion","Branch retinal vein occlusion","Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Branch retinal vein occlusion shows venous tortuosity and dilatation, flame-shaped and ‘dot-blot’ haemorrhages, and cotton-wool spots with retinal oedema (L19.2). The stem places the haemorrhages and cotton-wool spots in the superior temporal retina, which is one sector of the retina (not taken from the course material). The complications are chronic macular oedema and neovascularization (L19.2), and hypertension is one of the systemic predisposing factors (L19.2). Vein occlusion is taught from other angles in ophep-retina-14 and ophep-retina-22.\n\nCentral retinal vein occlusion is wrong because it shows extensive retinal haemorrhages and disc oedema, with acuity below 6/60 in the ischaemic form (L19.2), not changes confined to one sector.\n\nBranch retinal artery occlusion is wrong because its signs are variable visual acuity, retina whitening and arteriolar narrowing (L19.2), not haemorrhages with cotton-wool spots and macular oedema.\n\nAnterior ischemic optic neuropathy is wrong because its disc is swollen and pale with an altitudinal field defect (L21, slides 4, 5 and 9), and the lecture’s anterior ischemic optic neuropathy case presented with persistent headache, fatigue and weight loss and had a swollen right optic disc with flame-shaped haemorrhages around it (L31,32, slides 20, 22 and 23); this woman denies headache and fatigue, and the stem places the changes in the retina, not on the disc.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose branch retinal vein occlusion from sector-limited haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and macular oedema in a hypertensive patient.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.974" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-39", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 65-year-old woman comes to the emergency department due to seeing a sudden burst of flashing lights and blurred vision in her left eye. These symptoms started this morning. She now sees small spots in her field of vision. She felt \"like a curtain came down\" over her eye. She had a successful cataract extraction in her left eye 4 months ago. Her vital signs are stable. Examination shows a sluggish pupil. Ophthalmoscopy reveals retinal tears and a grayish-appearing retina. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Choroidal rupture","Retinal detachment","Central retinal artery occlusion","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"A sudden burst of flashing lights, small spots in the field and a curtain coming down, with retinal tears and a grayish retina, is retinal detachment. The key symptoms of rhegmatogenous detachment are floaters, flashes of light and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision, often sudden and rapid (L20, slide 11). It occurs when a tear or break develops in the retina and vitreous fluid seeps through it into the subretinal space (L20, slide 6), and in the fundus figure the detached retina is drawn pale grey (L20, slide 12). Cataract extraction is a recognised predisposition to detachment (not taken from the course material); the causes the lecture lists are aging, high myopia and eye trauma (L20, slide 6). Retinal detachment is among the vision-threatening ocular emergencies (L1,2, slide 135). The same picture in a 48-year-old is asked in ophep-retina-26.\n\nChoroidal rupture is wrong because it is a tear of the choroid after blunt injury (not taken from the course material), and the stem gives tears of the retina and no injury.\n\nCentral retinal artery occlusion is wrong because its findings are visual acuity below 6/60, a marked afferent pupillary defect, retinal whitening and a cherry-red spot (L19.2), and it does not come with flashes and retinal tears.\n\nProliferative diabetic retinopathy is wrong because it is neovascularization in a diabetic eye (L19.1), and the stem gives no diabetes. Detachment in that disease is the tractional type, with typically no initial tear (L20, slide 7), whereas this eye has retinal tears.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose rhegmatogenous retinal detachment from flashes, floaters, a curtain and retinal tears in a pseudophakic eye.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.976" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-40", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 65-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to a sudden loss of vision in his right eye. He has had diabetes, and has been treated with metformin and glyburide for the past 10 years. Visual acuity is reduced to light perception in his right eye, and normal in his left. His vital signs are normal. Ophthalmoscopy reveals loss of fundus details, floating debris and a dark red glow. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Retinal detachment","Diabetic retinopathy","Vitreous hemorrhage","Central retinal vein occlusion","Age related macular degeneration"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"This is vitreous haemorrhage in a diabetic; compare ophep-retina-37, where a long-standing diabetic with a dim red reflex and no fundus detail has the same diagnosis, and here the loss is more severe, down to light perception. Blood in the vitreous gives floating debris, hides fundus detail and leaves a dark red glow (not taken from the course material). Vitreous haemorrhage is listed among the causes of acute loss of vision (L1,2, slide 133). Long duration of diabetes is the first adverse risk factor listed for diabetic retinopathy (L19.1), and severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage is an indication for vitreoretinal surgery (L19.1).\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because it presents with floaters, flashes of light and a curtain or shadow in the peripheral vision (L20, slide 11), and the fundus in detachment shows the detached retina in grey against the orange attached retina (L20, slide 12), not a loss of fundus detail.\n\nDiabetic retinopathy is wrong as the final diagnosis because it is the underlying disease: its non-proliferative signs, microaneurysms and dot and blot haemorrhages, are seen on the fundus (L19.1), while the event described here is the bleed from proliferative disease.\n\nCentral retinal vein occlusion is wrong because it shows extensive retinal haemorrhages, engorged veins and disc oedema on the fundus (L19.2), and here no fundus detail can be seen.\n\nAge related macular degeneration is wrong because it is a disease of central vision, with a dry type that is a slow progressive atrophy (L35,36), and it does not hide the fundus (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose vitreous haemorrhage from sudden loss of vision in a diabetic with loss of fundus detail, floating debris and a dark red glow.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.978" },

{ id:"ophep-retina-41", bank:"endpoint", module:"ophtho", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"An 80-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to sudden loss of vision in his left eye that occurred on waking this morning. He has no associated pain. Past medical history is notable for hypertension. Current medications include ramipril and chlorthalidone. He has smoked half a pack of cigarettes a day for the past 60 years. His temperature is 36.8 C (98.4 F), blood pressure is 140/90 mm Hg, pulse is 86/min, and respirations are 14/min. Examination of the right eye shows no abnormalities. Fundoscopic examination of the left eye reveals swelling of the optic disc, retinal hemorrhages, dilated and tortuous veins, and cotton wool spots. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Acute anterior uveitis","Amaurosis fugax","Central retinal vein occlusion"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Sudden painless loss of vision with disc swelling, retinal haemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins and cotton wool spots is central retinal vein occlusion. The ischaemic form shows marked venous tortuosity and engorgement, extensive retinal haemorrhages, variable cotton wool spots and severe disc oedema (L19.2); the non-ischaemic form shows the same signs in a milder degree (L19.2). The predisposing factors are increasing age, hypertension, diabetes and abnormalities of coagulation, and raised intraocular pressure or periphlebitis in the eye (L19.2), and this man is 80 with hypertension. The same condition is asked in ophep-retina-22, in a 44-year-old, and in ophep-retina-14.\n\nAcute angle-closure glaucoma is wrong because it is listed among the important causes of a red eye (L1,2, slide 134), and this eye is painless; it does not produce retinal haemorrhages with dilated tortuous veins (not taken from the course material).\n\nAcute anterior uveitis is wrong because acute iritis is also listed among the causes of a red eye (L1,2, slide 134), and it is an inflammation of the front of the eye, not a cause of retinal haemorrhages and swollen disc (not taken from the course material).\n\nAmaurosis fugax is wrong because it is a transient loss of vision (not taken from the course material) that emboli may cause (L19.2), whereas this loss persisted since waking and comes with fundus signs of venous obstruction.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diagnose central retinal vein occlusion from painless sudden loss with disc swelling, haemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins and cotton wool spots.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.980" },

];
